  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper level low slowly builds into the region\ntoday and tomorrow, bringing several rounds of showers as \nshortwaves pivot around the longwave trough. Instability will \nreach its maximum today and some of the stronger showers will \nlikely contain thunder. Pea sized hail will be possible, and \nmaybe graupel in the higher terrain. The highest chances for \nshowers will be farther north where the better dynamics and \ncoldest air aloft will be. A lowering inversion heading into \nFriday should prevent any thunder chances, with instability only\nreaching up to around 10,000 feet and not significantly \nsurpassing -10 Celsius. However, the airmass will continue to \ncool and temperatures at the summits are expected to fall below \nfreezing on Friday, so a few flurries/snow showers will be \npossible in the mountains, especially farther north. Snow levels\nlook to drop to around 3,500-4,000 feet. Flow remains unblocked\nheading through Friday as the inversion will stay well above \nsummit level.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Surface high pressure builds in for the weekend,\nleading to a couple classic fall days. Highs will be in the 50s\nand 60s with mostly sunny skies. Radiational cooling conditions\nwill become somewhat favorable for Friday night, but some \nlingering boundary flow may prevent the most efficient \ntemperature decreases. The boundary layer looks to decouple at \nleast in the later part of the night though. Frost formation is \nexpected across most of the region, but it looks more uncertain \nin the Champlain Valley, the only area left in the growing \nseason. Temperatures there will be marginal and winds there will\nlikely hold on the longest.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: The remnants of Isaias will bring some rain to \nsouthern portions of our forecast area Sunday night into Monday.\nNot expecting any strong winds with this system. Northern \nextent of precipitation is uncertain. For now have highest \nchances for precipitation from Saranac Lake NY to Montpelier VT \nand areas south of there.",
